      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Every audit finds faults. A complaint, a near miss, a failed check. A working system closes them with proof. A paper one lets them drift open until the next audit. So the question is not how many fields a tool has. It is whether faults get closed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>CAPA means corrective and preventive action. It runs from raising a fault to closing it with proof.</li><li>A correction fixes the problem now. A corrective action fixes the root cause.</li><li>ISO 9001 and ISO 45001 both ask you to control nonconformities and deal with the root cause.</li><li>Quickler keeps the trail. It does not make you compliant with an ISO standard.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The point",
      "heading": "What CAPA is for",
      "html": "<p>Something is found. You find the root cause. You act to stop it happening again. You check the action worked. Then you close it.</p><p>Software does not do any of that. It keeps the trail.</p>"
